          body: 'An **adjective** is a word that describes or limits a noun or pronoun. It tells us *what kind*, *which one* or *how many*: a **rusty** gate, **this** road, **three** mangoes. Adjectives stand in two positions. Before the noun they are *attributive*: “a tired driver”. After a linking verb such as is, seems or felt (see the verbs lesson) they are *predicative*: “The driver felt **tired**.” In the second case the adjective describes the subject across the verb, which is why it is an adjective and not an adverb. Adjectives in English do not change for singular or plural: “a tall boy”, “tall boys”.',
